---
/**
 * t42 · Shell
 *
 * The oval as thick glass rather than a coloured shape.
 *
 * The wall is deep where the light enters and thin where it leaves, in four
 * struck steps rather than a falloff, so the upper left reads as glass with
 * depth behind it instead of a soft edge. That much is only a bevel with
 * better manners.
 *
 * What makes it an object is the far rim. On the shadow side the band goes
 * down through three steps — and then the outermost ring comes back up. That
 * is reflected light: the surface catching what the ground throws back at it,
 * which is the one thing a solid body never does and the one cue the eye
 * reads as glass without being told. It is also why this survives shrinking.
 * At 16 pixels the shading in most marks collapses into a single average; a
 * lit edge against a dark band is a hard boundary, and hard boundaries are
 * what a downsample keeps.
 *
 * The tail is part of the same field as the body — blended before rasterising,
 * tapering to two pixels — and the wall runs into it, thick on the lit flank,
 * lit again on the shadow edge. It is the thickest piece of glass in the
 * mark, which is exactly what a tail is: the place the form gathers before it
 * comes to a point.
 *
 * Ten violets, on a ramp whose dark half clears 4.5:1 against the white face.
 * The middle measures 5.18:1.
 */
import MarkFrame from '../MarkFrame.astro';
import { facePathsAt } from '../../../data/mark';

interface Props { size?: number }
const { size = 128 } = Astro.props;
---

